/-!
# No perfect cuboid has two edges in ratio `140 : 171`

Generated by `scripts/cuboid_class_cert.py` (do not edit by hand):

```
python3 scripts/cuboid_class_cert.py --sq 14,5
```

This is the fiber `(m, n) = (14, 5)`, with `U = 171`, `V = 140`, `W = 221`,
through the curve `E″` itself: moving a root to `0` makes it the pair curve of
`(p, q) = (U², V²) = (19600, 29241)` (sorted), with `D = q² − p² = 470876081`,
and a cuboid on the fiber gives a solution with `M² ≠ pq e²`
(`IsPerfectCuboid.not_onFiber_of_sq`). Unlike the pair `(2UV, W²)`, this pair has the
solutions `M² = pq e²` of the point `T₄` of order `4`, so the descent shows only that every
solution is of this kind (`sq_eq_of_isSol_of_casesOKT`). It has 32400 groups
`(λ, σ₀)` over 675 values of `λ`; 32322 of them are ruled out by their `2`-descent
class (`ClassKill`, with 260 distinct class tests), and the other 21964 cases are
split out, 21420 of them torsion translates and 80 of them by family TR (the
half-points of `±T₄` minus a point of order `8`, `trOK`). The kernel replays the certificate
with `classCert` (`decide +kernel`).

The curve `E″` has rank `0` and its `2`-Selmer group is its torsion modulo `2` (PARI/GP); this is
only the reason to expect the descent to work, and the proofs do not depend on it.

## Main results

* `Descent.sq_eq_fourteen_five` — the descent for the pair `(19600, 29241)`.
* `not_isPerfectCuboid_140_171` — no perfect cuboid has `171 X = 140 Z`.
-/

